# The Gandhian Era: Mass Struggles to Independence

> From his return in January 1915 to the midnight of 15 August 1947,
> Gandhi turned satyagraha into the engine of a mass movement — Champaran,
> Non-Cooperation, Dandi, and Quit India — while the Cabinet Mission,
> Direct Action, and Partition closed the era at a terrible price.

## 1. The Method and the Early Satyagrahas

Gandhi returned from **South Africa on 9 January 1915**, seasoned by two
decades of satyagraha — the Phoenix settlement, Tolstoy Farm, and campaigns
for Indian rights. **Satyagraha** (truth-force) and **ahimsa** (non-violence)
converted moral pressure into political technique.

| Satyagraha | Year | Cause |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Champaran | 1917 | Indigo planters' tinkathia system; with Rajendra Prasad |
| Ahmedabad | 1918 | Mill workers' dispute — his first hunger strike |
| Kheda | 1918 | Tax remission after crop failure; with Vallabhbhai Patel |

[definition]
**Tinkathia** — the Champaran rule forcing tenants to grow indigo on
three-twentieths of their land for European planters. Gandhi's inquiry
there won the system's abolition — satyagraha's first Indian victory.
[/definition]

## 2. Rowlatt and Jallianwala Bagh (1919)

The **Rowlatt Act** (March 1919) allowed detention without trial; Gandhi's
hartal of 6 April spread. In Punjab, General **Dyer** ordered firing on an
unarmed **Baisakhi** crowd at **Jallianwala Bagh, Amritsar, on 13 April
1919** — hundreds killed. The **Hunter Commission** censured Dyer mildly;
Tagore returned his knighthood and Gandhi his Kaiser-i-Hind medal. Trust in
British justice never recovered.

## 3. Khilafat and Non-Cooperation (1920-1922)

Gandhi allied with the **Khilafat movement** (Ali brothers) defending the
Ottoman Caliph, and launched **Non-Cooperation** — ratified at the **Nagpur
session (December 1920)** after the special Calcutta session:

- Surrender of titles and honours; boycott of councils, courts, and
  government schools.
- Boycott of foreign cloth — a million charkhas spun India's own.
- The **Tilak Swaraj Fund** oversubscribed; the Prince of Wales's visit
  (November 1921) met empty streets.

At **Chauri Chaura (5 February 1922)** a crowd burnt a police station with
policemen inside; Gandhi halted the movement at Bardoli (12 February) rather
than let violence define it — and was jailed for six years a month later.

[important title="Why withdraw at the peak?"]
Gandhi insisted non-violence was the movement's very soul, not a tactic:
a violent mass movement would hand the state a licence for repression and
poison the future nation. Critics then and since have called it a blunder;
supporters, the discipline that made the next three campaigns possible.
[/important]

## 4. Simon to Purna Swaraj (1927-1930)

The all-white **Simon Commission (1927-28)** was greeted with "Simon, go
back" — Lala Lajpat Rai was fatally injured in a Lahore lathi charge, and
Bhagat Singh answered with Saunders's killing. The **Nehru Report (1928)**
drafted a constitution with dominion status; **Jinnah's Fourteen Points
(1929)** marked the League's parting of ways. At **Lahore (December 1929)**,
under Jawaharlal's presidency, Congress voted **Purna Swaraj** — complete
independence — and observed **26 January 1930** as the first Independence
Day.

## 5. Civil Disobedience and the Round Tables (1930-1934)

The **Dandi March (12 March - 6 April 1930)**: Gandhi walked **240 miles**
from Sabarmati to the sea with 78 followers and broke the salt law, "shaking
the foundations of the British Empire" for eight annas of tax.

- Spread: C. Rajagopalachari's Vedaranyam salt march; Khan Abdul Ghaffar
  Khan's Khudai Khidmatgars in the North-West Frontier; Chandra Singh
  Garhwali's soldiers refusing to fire on crowds at Peshawar; women led by
  Sarojini Naidu at the Dharasana raid.
- **First Round Table Conference (1930):** Congress absent. **Gandhi-Irwin
  Pact (5 March 1931)** paused the movement; Gandhi attended the **Second
  RTC (1931)** as sole Congress delegate — it failed over minorities.
- **Communal Award (August 1932)** gave separate electorates to the
  Depressed Classes; Gandhi's fast unto death in Yerawada jail produced the
  **Poona Pact (September 1932)**: joint electorates with reserved seats.

| Conference | Date | Congress role |
| --- | --- | --- |
| First RTC | Nov 1930 - Jan 1931 | Boycotted |
| Second RTC | Sept - Dec 1931 | Gandhi, sole delegate |
| Third RTC | Nov - Dec 1932 | Boycotted |

## 6. Act of 1935, Quit India, and the INA

The **Government of India Act 1935** created elected provincial ministries
(1937-39 — Congress ministries resigned over the war) and a federation that
never came. After the failed **Cripps Mission (1942)**, the **Quit India
resolution of 8 August 1942** in Bombay set Gandhi's "**Do or Die**":

- Leaders arrested within hours; underground leaders like **Aruna Asaf
  Ali** hoisted the flag at Gowalia Tank; **Usha Mehta** ran a secret
  radio.
- **Parallel governments:** Ballia under **Chittu Pandey**, Tamluk's Jatiya
  Sarkar, and Satara's Prati Sarkar of Nana Patil.

**Subhas Chandra Bose** — elected Congress president in 1938 but outmanoeuvred
at Tripuri (1939) — escaped India in 1941, reached Berlin, then Singapore:
the **Azad Hind Government (1943)**, the **INA** with its Rani Jhansi
Regiment, and "**Delhi Chalo**." The INA's defeats at **Imphal and Kohima
(1944)** were followed by the **Red Fort trials (1945-46)** of Shah Nawaz,
Sehgal, and Dhillon — which turned the accused into national heroes and
shook the loyalty of the Indian armed forces (the RIN mutiny of February
1946).

## 7. Cabinet Mission to Partition (1945-1947)

@diagram tree id="road-to-partition" layout="tree" aria="Steps from the Cabinet Mission to independence in 1947"
  Road to Freedom -> "Cabinet Mission, March 1946"
  Road to Freedom -> "Direct Action Day, 16 Aug 1946"
  Road to Freedom -> "Mountbatten Plan, 3 June 1947"
  Road to Freedom -> "Independence and Partition, 14-15 Aug 1947"
  Cabinet Mission, March 1946 -> "Grouping plan; Constituent Assembly elected"
  Direct Action Day, 16 Aug 1946 -> "Calcutta killings; Noakhali; Gandhi's march"
  Mountbatten Plan, 3 June 1947 -> "Partition accepted by Congress and League"
  Independence and Partition, 14-15 Aug 1947 -> "Radcliffe Line; two dominions"
@/diagram

The **Lahore Resolution of the League (23 March 1940)** had demanded
separate states — the **two-nation theory** Jinnah championed. After the
**Cabinet Mission's** grouping plan failed and the League's **Direct Action
Day (16 August 1946)** lit the Calcutta killings, Partition became
inevitable. The **Mountbatten Plan (3 June 1947)** divided the Punjab and
Bengal; the **Radcliffe Line** was drawn in five weeks and announced on
**17 August**, after the transfer of power at midnight on **14-15 August
1947** — Nehru's "tryst with destiny" spoken amid a displacement of some
ten million people.

[note]
India and Pakistan became two dominions under the Indian Independence Act
(18 July 1947). Gandhi spent 15 August fasting in Calcutta — independence
arrived "wrapped in the shroud of Partition."
[/note]

## Key Takeaways

- Gandhi's local satyagrahas (Champaran 1917, Ahmedabad and Kheda 1918)
  proved the method before the national stage.
- Jallianwala (1919), Chauri Chaura (1922), Dandi (1930), and Quit India
  (1942) mark the rhythm of mass struggle and its costs.
- Institutional battles ran alongside: Nehru Report vs Fourteen Points,
  three Round Tables, the 1932 Poona Pact, and the 1935 Act.
- The INA, the RIN mutiny, Cabinet Mission failure, Direct Action Day, and
  the June 3 Plan delivered independence — and Partition — in 1947.
